<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Song Jiaoren</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1882–1913</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Jiaoren was a Chinese revolutionary and a founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>, which he led to victory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s first national parliamentary elections in 1912–1913. He championed a cabinet system that would limit presidential power. He was shot at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> railway station in March 1913, an assassination widely blamed on President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a>, which led to the failed '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-revolution">Second Revolution</a>'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/song-jiaoren.jpg" alt="Song Jiaoren" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sung_Chiao-jen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
